17:43:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're making a very fair point: if what you learned in school says water only turns to vapor at 100°C, then everything I've been saying sounds like complicated nonsense. Let me clear that up directly. ### The confusion: boiling vs. evaporation In school, we're often taught that **water boils at 100°C** and turns into steam. That is true. But we're rarely taught that **evaporation is different from boiling**. Boiling is when the entire liquid reaches a temperature where vapor forms bubbles throughout. Evaporation is a surface process that happens at any temperature. Your school probably didn't teach you that because it's a subtlety that gets glossed over. But you have seen evaporation your whole life—you just didn't call it that. ### Three everyday examples you can't deny 1. **Sweat cooling your skin** – Your body is 37°C, well below 100°C. Yet sweat evaporates and cools you. If evaporation only happened at 100°C, you'd never cool down by sweating. 2. **A puddle drying on a warm day** – The sun heats the puddle to maybe 30°C, not 100°C. Yet it dries up. That's evaporation. 3. **A glass of water left out for a week** – Leave a glass of water on your counter at room temperature (20°C). Mark the level. After a week, it's lower. The water didn't boil—it evaporated. ### So why did your fridge experiment show no loss? Because evaporation is **slow** at low temperatures and **stops** if the air above the water is still and nearly saturated. Your fridge had still air, so net evaporation was too small to see over two weeks. That doesn't mean evaporation didn't happen at all—it means it was balanced by condensation, or the rate was below what you could measure. ### The bottom line - Water does **not** need to be 100°C to evaporate. - Boiling is a violent, bulk process at 100°C. - Evaporation is a gentle, surface process at any temperature. - Your school taught you one part of the picture; real life shows you the rest. If you still doubt, try the glass-of-water-on-your-counter test at room temperature. You will see it drop over a week. That's not hypothetical mumbo-jumbo—that's something you can see with your own eyes.
